  <p>
    Surveys consistently rank public speaking as the <strong>number one fear in the
    world</strong> — above heights, spiders, and, famously, death itself. If the thought
    of standing up to speak makes your heart race, you are not broken and you are not
    alone. You're having a completely normal response to a situation your nervous system
    misreads as danger.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the fear is so common</h2>
  <p>
    Public speaking triggers a threat response because, evolutionarily, being scrutinized
    by a group meant your standing in the tribe was at stake. Your body responds with the
    same fight-or-flight chemistry it would use for physical danger: racing heart, shallow
    breath, shaking hands. The problem isn't the feeling — it's that we interpret the
    feeling as proof that something will go wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    The single biggest reason the fear persists is simple:
    <strong>almost no one actually practices.</strong> We rehearse a presentation once or
    twice in our heads and call it preparation. But speaking is a physical skill, like a
    free throw or a guitar chord. You don't get better by thinking about it. You get
    better by doing reps.
  </p>

  <h2>Why "just practice" is harder than it sounds</h2>
  <p>Practice is the right advice. It's also the part everyone skips, for three reasons:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It feels awkward.</strong> Talking to an empty room feels strange, so we avoid it.</li>
    <li><strong>It needs an audience.</strong> Rounding up friends to listen is high-friction and a little embarrassing.</li>
    <li><strong>It gives no feedback.</strong> Without an outside observer, you can't see your own habits, so practice feels aimless.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Remove those three barriers and practice becomes something you'll actually do. That's
    the entire idea behind SpeechTeacher: a private room, an instant prompt, and objective
    feedback the moment you stop.
  </p>

  <h2>A practice loop that works</h2>
  <p>Here's a simple, repeatable loop you can run in under two minutes a day:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Take a prompt.</strong> Don't plan. The goal is to train thinking on your feet, not reciting.</li>
    <li><strong>Speak for 60 seconds.</strong> Out loud, at full voice. Whispering doesn't count — your body needs the real reps.</li>
    <li><strong>Review one metric.</strong> Pick a single thing to improve — pace, or filler words — and look only at that.</li>
    <li><strong>Repeat tomorrow.</strong> Consistency beats intensity. Five one-minute reps across a week beat one long session.</li>
  </ul>
  <blockquote>
    Confidence isn't the thing that lets you start speaking. Speaking is the thing that
    builds confidence. The order matters.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>What to expect over time</h2>
  <p>
    The racing heart never fully disappears — and that's fine, because skilled speakers
    feel it too. What changes is your <strong>relationship</strong> to it. After a few
    weeks of short daily reps, the sensation stops reading as "something is wrong" and
    starts reading as "I'm about to do a thing I've done a hundred times." That
    reinterpretation is the whole game, and it only comes from volume.
  </p>
  <p>
    You don't need a stage, an audience, or a coach to start. You need a prompt and sixty
    seconds. <a href="/practice">Open the trainer</a> and take your first rep right now.
  </p>
